  "query_preview": "..Early morning satellite imagery showed scattered\nclouds across SE Arizona in SW flow aloft out ahead of an upper\nlevel trof that extended from NE AZ to SW AZ. Radar showed spotty\nlight showers and a rumble or two of thunder were moving NE \naround 20-25 mph across the region with rainfall totals with \nthese showers mostly less than 0.05\". These spotty showers, \nrumbles of thunder, will continue for the rest of this morning \nbefore becoming more scattered late in the morning into this \nafternoon as atmosphere destabilizes with daytime heating. Some\nof the showers/thunderstorms will be a bit frisky, especially \neast of Tucson, with gusty winds to 40 mph and brief heavy rain.\n\nTuesday: lingering moisture combined with daytime heating will\nsupport mostly isolated showers from Tucson east. \n\nThe upper low off the PacNW this morning will be moving down the\nwest coast Tuesday and Wednesday before swinging across the\ndesert southwest Thursday and Friday. Scattered showers on \nWednesday will become more widespread on Thursday. Brief heavy \nrain is the main threat Wednesday afternoon into Thursday with \nWPC having portions of the area under either a marginal or slight\nrisk (northeast of Tucson) of flash flooding within 25 miles of\na point.\n\nMostly dry Friday into the weekend, although there is a small\nchance (10%) for showers along the AZ/NM border. \n\nTemperatures today through Wednesday will be at or slightly \nabove normal, then cooler than normal Thursday into the upcoming\nweekend.",
